  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health Support to Displacement and War-Affected Communities in Zaporizka Oblast</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The war in Ukraine triggered one of the largest humanitarian crises on record with over 5 million people displaced as of May 2023, with another 4.7 million people reported to have already return to their habitual place of residence (IOM General Population Survey, May 2023). While the warfare continues, physical damage to more than 1,500 health facilities has been registered since the start of the full-scale invasion in February 2022 (RDNA2, World Bank, 2023). Simultaneously, the demand for health services and medicines has increased due to incidence of war-related injuries, exacerbation of chronic diseases, and the greater risk of diseases such as tuberculosis, HIV, cholera. 
 
In coordination with the Government of Ukraine (GoU) and local partners, IOM aims to increase access to health care services for internally displaced persons (IDPs) and war-affected communities in Zaporizka Oblast in a manner that upholds their rights, dignity and well-being, through providing essential equipment, medical supplies and medicines to health facilities, and developing capacities of medical practitioners. The project contributes to enhancing resilience and recovery of displacement- and war-affected communities in Zaporizka Oblast through improving the access to health care services.</narrative>
  </description>
